FILE CONTROL-LAW-FAST-LOOK-2026-09-10.md 
 **FAST_LOOK_TWO_ANCHORS — best-known coarse-search gap: +113.4% pooled efficiency over the declared law on two anchors; the gain looks mostly reachable by a per-beam local rule, with cross-beam search adding only about 2.1% over the local probe.**

`DIAGNOSTIC_NOT_CLAIM` · `FAST_LOOK_TWO_ANCHORS`

This is a two-anchor shape check, not an answer to the control-law question. It reports the best-known values found by one coarse search. It must not be promoted beyond this diagnostic; the companion eight-anchor evaluation under both provisioning rules is the relevant larger run.

## Result

The declared law was not close to the settings found in this fast look. With the committed association held exactly fixed, pooled efficiency rose from **45.375 to 96.836 Mbit/J**, or **+113.412% relative to the declared law**. Equivalently, the declared value is **53.142% below** the best-known coarse-search value.

Every accepted searched setting preserved at least the declared credited spectral efficiency for every active transmission slot. This is stronger than the unchanged service guard: served count therefore stayed at 100/100 on each anchor, and rate-target attainment increased from 72 to 100 users on anchor 0 and from 70 to 100 on anchor 1.

| FAST_LOOK_TWO_ANCHORS | Declared EE (Mbit/J) | Best found (Mbit/J) | Best found over declared | Declared below best found | Served, declared → found | 50 Mbit/s target attained, declared → found |
|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| 2025-11-04, step 0 | 46.715 | 100.727 | +115.620% | 53.622% | 100 → 100 | 72 → 100 |
| 2025-11-04, step 1 | 44.100 | 93.086 | +111.081% | 52.625% | 100 → 100 | 70 → 100 |
| Two-anchor pool | 45.375 | 96.836 | +113.412% | 53.142% | 200 → 200 | 142 → 200 |

The improvement came primarily from more productive mode choices rather than energy reduction. Pooled integrated bits increased **115.265%**, while joules increased **0.868%**. Per anchor, the changes were +118.873% bits / +1.509% joules at step 0 and +111.626% bits / +0.258% joules at step 1.

## What was searched

The fixed assignment was the corrected pipeline's final committed `ORACLE_SET` configuration at each anchor. No user was moved, dropped, or reattached. The declared comparison was reconstructed with corrected `MARGIN_Q` provisioning, `Gamma_r(n_b) / q_0.10`, and matched the existing corrected receipt to binary64 tolerance for both bits and joules.

For every one of the 48 realised boundaries and every exact rational TDM subslot, the search varied each simultaneously active beam's radiated power on this grid relative to that beam's declared power:

`0, 0.25, 0.375, 0.5, 0.625, 0.75, 0.875, 1.0, 1.125, 1.25, 1.5, 2.0 × declared power`, with every value clipped to the unchanged **1.65 W** per-beam cap and **1.65 W** also included explicitly.

At every power candidate, all **28 unchanged ACM table modes**, plus no transmission, were evaluated; the highest-throughput mode that decoded under the realised SINR was used. This is an offline best-known diagnostic and has realised-boundary information that a deployable causal rule would not automatically possess.

The optimizer used up to four deterministic coordinate sweeps per subslot and three efficiency-ratio updates per anchor. Each coordinate trial recomputed the complete coupled interference matrix, realised SINR for every active beam, the nonlinear PA draw, per-chain circuit draw, and per-satellite baseband draw. It made 3,593,726 power/mode comparisons at step 0 and 1,333,070 at step 1.

| FAST_LOOK_TWO_ANCHORS search constraint | Unchanged value or treatment |
|---|---|
| Assignment | Exact committed `ORACLE_SET` mapping, held fixed |
| Provisioning baseline | Corrected `MARGIN_Q`: `Gamma_r(n_b) / q_0.10` |
| Power cap | 1.65 W per beam |
| Service guard | Selection-time served count ≥ 60 at step 0 and ≥ 47 at step 1; searched results served 100 at both |
| Rate target | Unchanged 50 Mbit/s; attainment reported separately |
| Extra conservative acceptance condition | No active transmission slot may receive less credited spectral efficiency than under the declared law |
| Interference | Full simultaneous cross-beam coupling recomputed exactly at every candidate |
| Integration | Unchanged 48-boundary trapezoidal integration over 47 × 0.640 s |

## Does it need cross-beam information?

**Mostly no, on these two anchors.** An independent local probe—each beam choosing from the same grid using its own decoded rate and PA/chain cost while holding other beams at their declared powers—reached **94.796 Mbit/J** pooled, +108.915% over the declared law and only **2.107% below** the coupled search's 96.836 Mbit/J. The large gain therefore looks predominantly per-beam-local; exact cross-beam information appears useful for the last roughly two per cent, not for the main effect. This is a rough two-anchor judgement, not a proof.

## Provenance and timing

| FAST_LOOK_TWO_ANCHORS provenance | Value |
|---|---|
| Real anchor IDs | `V025_CEILING30/date/2025-11-04:step/0`; `V025_CEILING30/date/2025-11-04:step/1` |
| World SHA-256 | `cb1c594d852a5aba1bda7ce26b2b1604c97d77ae5a6908048cd3ea5c7dda8e1b` |
| Training seed | `1914058825465947281` |
| Step-0 fixed-assignment SHA-256 | `b23418c436871d9cc193e15d052079fd9d77902a9947461207d193e267ce735a` |
| Step-1 fixed-assignment SHA-256 | `fbbb05709a5018b3111b8555bef9e73f05a62c81e88ef302821b53fd27db70cc` |
| Measured run wall time | 131.859 s (2 min 11.859 s) |
| Concurrency | One process; numerical-library threads pinned to one |

No sealed constant, threshold, sign, seed, horizon, price, guard, acceptance rule, or declared control law was changed. The diagnostic only rebuilt the matching world, replayed the declared corrected law, and evaluated alternative power/mode controls outside the pipeline.

With more time, I would use a denser absolute-plus-relative power grid, multiple coupled-search starting points, and separate causal-mode and realised-information searches across the larger anchor panel. That would test grid sensitivity and distinguish deployable local headroom from offline mode-selection headroom.


FILE CONTROL-CEILING-FRAMING-2026-09-10.md 
 Not yet well posed for the intended decision: define C2 by an explicit causal, service-matched policy contract before treating its gap as deployable headroom.

`DIAGNOSTIC_NOT_CLAIM` — Design review, 2026-09-10.

The fixed-assignment experiment is worth doing. Its present specification does not determine what “best achievable” means well enough to support a research pivot. The single repair is to specify the admissible controller: its observations, decision times, protected service obligations and evaluation objective. Retain a more informed oracle as a separately labelled diagnostic. Do not silently strengthen the sealed guard or change the production law.

I read the [running job’s prompt](/home/sat/mcrl-v023-codex-audits/parallel-20260909/codex-controlceiling.md), the relevant source and existing diagnostic evidence. This review does not certify the running optimizer or its eventual number. No measurement or training was launched and no sealed material was modified.

**Fixed assignment is a useful isolation, with a conditional answer.** Let `J(A, π)` be pooled bits/joules for assignment `A` and a complete power/mode policy `π`, on the same physical workload and service contract. The question is:

`max_π J(A₀, π) − J(A₀, π₀)`.

That measures control headroom conditional on the assignment the pipeline committed. It does not measure all control headroom available after reassociation. Conversely, optimizing association under `π₀` measures association headroom conditional on that law. Holding a law fixed means rerunning that law for each assignment, including its power/interference solve; it does not mean retaining the old numeric powers.

Neither conditional gain is generally additive. Two separately useless changes can work together; two individually useful changes can recover the same waste. For example, efficiencies `J(A₀,π₀)=J(A₁,π₀)=J(A₀,π₁)=1` and `J(A₁,π₁)=2` give zero separate gains and a doubled joint result. With the three nonbaseline entries all equal to 2, adding the separate gains double-counts the improvement. These are illustrative counterexamples, not project measurements. Relative percentages also cannot simply be added along a sequential intervention.

The honest wording is: “On these committed assignments, under this information and service contract, the declared law leaves at least X recoverable by the evaluated control alternative.” Replace “at least” with a certified interval only when justified. A subsequent association search under the improved law can expose interaction; it supplies another feasible joint result unless globally certified. A small fixed-assignment gap cannot establish that the system is bounded by physics. Delete that inference from the runner’s rules.

There is an additional mismatch in the existing comparison vocabulary. The recent association runner selects using `B − η_ref E` at boundary zero, then reports the committed 48-boundary endpoint. Its [source records both objectives and the horizon distinction](/home/sat/mcrl-v025-arch-ws/scripts/run_prefix_ablation.py:995). A local certificate for that fixed-price snapshot score is not a certificate for pooled committed efficiency. The reported roughly one per cent is not, merely by being attached to a certified local optimum or an exhaustive bounded catalogue, the association layer’s entire room under the project’s objective. Align objective, horizon, starting assignment and search domain before comparing the two layers.

**The ratio is legitimate; its domain is currently underspecified.** For a fixed panel with declared exposure weights `wₐ`, the finite-panel objective is:

`η(π) = Σₐ wₐ Bₐ(Aₐ,π) / Σₐ wₐ Eₐ(Aₐ,π)`.

For deployment, the corresponding long-run target is expected bits divided by expected joules under the deployment distribution. Neither is a mean of per-beam efficiencies, a mean of anchor percentage gains, or an expectation of individual ratios. Report the two pooled totals and `η₂/η₁ − 1`; state the weights and retain every scheduled anchor.

Even maximizing each anchor’s own ratio and then pooling does not generally maximize the pooled ratio. A common fractional-programming parameter `λ` gives the auxiliary objective `Σ wₐ(Bₐ − λEₐ)`, with its optimum zero at the optimal pooled ratio. A single solve at the project’s frozen `η_ref` does not certify ratio optimality; the optimizers can coincide without making the criteria equivalent. Use `λ` only inside a separate diagnostic optimizer; do not replace the sealed price. Fractional programming supplies a reformulation, not automatic convexity or a global certificate. [Shen and Yu, Part I, Section II](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1802.10192).

Fix the roster, physical beam identities, geometry, bandwidth/colour allocation, TDM order and shares, inventory, exposure duration and interruption accounting. Then specify whether power and mode are chosen once per anchor, per boundary, or per scheduled transmission. The existing [TDM implementation schedules different users within a beam](/home/sat/mcrl-v025-ladder-ws/src/mcrl/physics_v025/architectures.py:325). “Per-beam settings” alone does not identify the action space. Allowing the optimizer to reschedule airtime or blank slots introduces scheduling authority; it must not enter unnoticed.

With finite modes, bounded powers, a nonempty closed service-feasible domain and positive energy, an appropriate finite-scenario formulation can attain a finite optimum. The ratio itself is not the obstacle. But all-dark operation gives `0/0`, which the [endpoint explicitly marks undefined](/home/sat/mcrl-v025-ladder-ws/src/mcrl/physics_v025/endpoint.py:90). Infeasible anchors and undefined outcomes must be reported, not discarded or converted into favourable efficiencies. Stochastic service also needs a stated horizon and reliability criterion; a positive rate target cannot generally be guaranteed through every arbitrarily deep fade.

**The guard does not prevent the service tradeoff the question worries about.** The current comparison guard is [selection-time served count at least BASELINE’s count](/home/sat/mcrl-v025-arch-ws/scripts/run_prefix_ablation.py:712). It is not a promise to preserve the committed arm’s users, its higher served count, or its delivered rates. In the corrected endpoint, “served” means positive decoding time; rate attainment separately requires integrated bits at least `r* × duration`. [Endpoint definitions](/home/sat/mcrl-v025-ladder-ws/src/mcrl/physics_v025/adapter.py:289).

Consequently, unchanged association can coexist with deliberately causing outages. If C1 serves more users than BASELINE, a count-only guard can permit serving fewer than C1. Even a count floor equal to C1 permits exchanging identities when other assigned users can be restored. Preserving every served identity still permits near-starvation if any positive decoding suffices. A boundary-zero guard does not guarantee service across the committed interval.

Dropping an expensive user can increase bits/joule whenever the energy saved outweighs the lost bits at the current pooled efficiency; reduced interference can additionally help others. This is an objective tradeoff, not an optimizer bug. Assignment equality does not prohibit it.

“Unchanged rate target” must therefore distinguish three things: leaving `r*` unchanged as a provisioning parameter, enforcing nominal SINR feasibility, and delivering the required bits to each protected user over the horizon. They are different constraints. If C2 requires all assigned users to achieve `r*` while C1 does not, C1 may lie outside C2’s feasible set. That comparison can describe a better service outcome, but it is not an incumbent optimality gap over a common feasible set. If the cap makes the target impossible, report infeasibility; do not quietly substitute the lowest mode or discard those users.

Preserve the original guard result as requested, but do not call it a same-service improvement. Alongside it, use a separately labelled comparison with a protected roster and explicit rate/reliability obligations chosen before optimization. Report per-user bits, identities lost and gained, target attainment and outage duration in both. A paired service audit is an evaluation outcome, not permission to expose future outcomes to the controller. If the gain disappears with comparable service, the finding is about the permitted service tradeoff.

**The full problem is nonconvex, but an important inner problem is tractable.** The source uses discrete commanded ACM modes and threshold decoding. It also charges PA supply power as `sqrt(p × p_sat)/η_max`, which is increasing and concave in radiated power, rather than an affine power cost. Thus the usual concave-throughput/convex-energy assumptions do not hold here. [Causal ACM](/home/sat/mcrl-v025-ladder-ws/src/mcrl/physics_v025/acm.py:73), [PA supply](/home/sat/mcrl-v025-ladder-ws/src/mcrl/physics_v025/energy.py:33). Even a Shannon replacement would not generally remove the nonconvexity caused by coupled interference.

However, fix the active transmissions, required decoded modes and time shares in one frozen channel state. Their SINR constraints have the form:

`pᵢ ≥ γᵢ(Nᵢ + Σⱼ≠ᵢ Gᵢⱼpⱼ)/gᵢ`, or `p ≥ Fp + u`.

For the ordinary nonnegative coupling model with positive noise and direct gains, if the spectral radius `ρ(F)<1`, `p*=(I−F)⁻¹u` is the componentwise least feasible power. It must also satisfy every cap. Because the actual PA cost increases in every component, this least vector minimizes energy for those fixed successful modes despite the concave PA formula. This conclusion follows from the displayed constraints; standard interference-control theory also supports distributed fixed-point methods under its stated conditions. [Yates, 1995](https://web.stanford.edu/class/ee360/previous/suppRead/read1/uplink_pc_cellular.pdf).

That observation can make a finite mode search much more informative than a generic continuous optimizer. It does not solve free outage selection, multiple fading scenarios, horizon-level service constraints or restricted causal feedback policies. Enumerating modes alone is insufficient if decode/outage patterns or scheduling remain unaccounted for. A clipped fixed point can converge while violating the target; convergence is not feasibility. Check achieved SINRs and service explicitly.

Report a feasible incumbent `L` and a valid upper bound `U`, or report only “best known.” A large feasible improvement establishes existence under the tested assumptions. A small best-known improvement establishes little about remaining room. A ceiling requires coverage or bounds over the entire declared action space, including the inner solve. Include C1 as a candidate whenever it is feasible. A worse search result then diagnoses search failure, not negative headroom.

**Common fading and causal decisions are separate requirements.** Both policies must see the same exogenous desired-link and cross-link fading, keyed by physical identity and time. Recompute each policy’s interference using its own powers. Sharing the realised interference totals would incorrectly suppress the intervention itself. The [tape builds realised cross-gains from physical links and fading](/home/sat/mcrl-v025-ladder-ws/src/mcrl/physics_v025/tapes.py:388).

Do not evaluate one policy at nominal gain and the other at its design quantile. Do not let the alternative select a mode from realised future SINR while the reference selects from its causal view. The corrected codec [chooses the transmitted mode first and credits that mode only if it decodes](/home/sat/mcrl-v025-ladder-ws/src/mcrl/physics_v025/acm.py:73); substituting whichever mode would have decoded recreates the wrong reference.

Common random numbers alone do not fix oracle information. Optimizing directly on the same full future tape used for scoring permits hindsight even when C1 is evaluated on that identical tape. Current channel measurements are legitimate only if obtainable before the relevant transmission at the claimed precision and latency. Full geometry may be predictable; future fades and future outcome labels are not thereby observable.

Distinguish a hindsight oracle, a controller restricted to declared pre-decision observations, and an implemented local or coordinating rule. A certified hindsight optimum bounds the specified simulated problem; an uncertified hindsight search is merely a hindsight best-known result. Its gap is not promised deployable gain. Freeze the causal rule before evaluation on additional fading realisations and unused evaluation worlds. Neither memorizing anchor IDs nor fitting and scoring on the same eight anchors demonstrates a reusable policy.

The existing [common-fading audit](/home/sat/mcrl-v025-arch-ws/CONVEXITY-ADVERSARIAL-2026-09-10.md:98) already shows that channel evaluation and credited traffic can alter the apparent preferred configuration. It is a relevant warning, not evidence that this running gap will vanish. If common fading erases the gain, the original number was an unmatched comparison, not control headroom. Also, a desired-fade quantile is not a percentile of coupled SINR: realised interference is random too. Dividing a nominal target by `q` does not by itself guarantee 90% network availability or target delivery.

**A gap confined to cap-binding anchors is a conditional finding, not a general control breakthrough.** Define cap strata from C1 before examining improvement. Report each stratum’s prevalence, pooled bits/joules, service changes and share of the total gain, under the corrected rule as well as the original. The relevant question is how often that regime occurs in deployment and how much aggregate benefit remains under that exposure mix. Oversampling adverse anchors can produce an impressive panel number with little operational value.

For an additive accounting decomposition, use `ΔBₛ − η₀ΔEₛ` for each disjoint stratum, with the same whole-panel reference efficiency `η₀`. These terms sum to `E₁(η₁−η₀)`. Averaging stratum percentages does not give that decomposition. Beam-level correlations are descriptive: coupled interference prevents attributing a unique causal gain to each beam without an explicit intervention.

A cap-only gap may justify a cap-aware fallback, a revised feasibility/admission rule, or reassociation of an impossible fixed assignment. It can still matter if sufficiently prevalent. It does not establish a broad learned coordination opportunity. If comparable service is infeasible at the fixed assignment, that is also evidence that the layer isolation excludes a necessary remedy.

**Both provisioning rules need exact identities.** If assignment, admissible controls, physical evaluator, information and service constraints are identical, freely optimizing power and mode should give the same C2 optimum regardless of which provisioning rule supplied the initial guess. The two rules then supply different C1 references, not different physics ceilings. If each rule commits a different assignment or supplies a different BASELINE guard, separate conditional optima are legitimate, but their difference also includes those conditioning changes. If C2 remains constrained to a rule-specific power formula, call it a restricted policy-family search. A large gap confined to the defective rule measures defect cost, not residual opportunity in the corrected system.

Do not treat “corrected” as a sufficient version identifier. Existing evidence distinguishes simple `Γ/q` division from an additional clearance implementation, with different committed results. [Provisioning audit](/home/sat/mcrl-v023-codex-audits/parallel-20260909/PROVISIONING-ADVERSARIAL-2026-09-10.md:94). Record source hashes, imported paths, configuration mappings, anchor/tape identities, rule variant and numerical residuals. Reproduce the declared law through the diagnostic evaluator before interpreting any alternative. The [recent runner imports a sibling source tree explicitly](/home/sat/mcrl-v025-arch-ws/scripts/run_prefix_ablation.py:34); inspecting only its workspace’s apparent source can review the wrong implementation.

Threshold fragility matters here. Check physical SINR clearance and sensitivity to finite power resolution, channel-estimation error and control delay. A policy landing infinitesimally above an ACM threshold can exploit numerical precision without producing a robust link margin. Keep numerical correction distinct from changing a physical margin. Recheck all coupled beams after each update.

**A large valid gap justifies investigating the control law; it does not justify three learned components.** First test how much is recovered by a corrected margin rule, cap-aware mode choice and deterministic interference-control or small mode-search baselines. The current control solve already uses `coupling @ power`; cross-beam dependence is not newly discovered authority. [Existing solve](/home/sat/mcrl-v025-ladder-ws/src/mcrl/physics_v025/batch.py:366).

A better rule based on a beam’s own state and measured local interference makes this a control-law redesign. Dependence on joint state establishes a coordination requirement only when materially different actions are needed for states indistinguishable under the proposed local observations. Merely inspecting the optimizer’s global input matrix does not prove that necessity. Nor does strong coupling preclude a distributed analytic rule using measurements or messages.

Learning becomes a supported direction only if a practically meaningful residual survives strong nonlearned baselines, the deciding information is available before action, the policy generalizes, and inference plus communication fits the operational deadline. Compare service violations, tail latency and total cost as well as mean efficiency. Learning can be useful to approximate an expensive optimizer, estimate an unobserved quantity or handle model mismatch; those are specific hypotheses to test. Nothing in a large oracle gap selects three components, their interfaces or their training targets. Replace the prompt’s inference from joint knowledge to a learned coordinating component with this evidence requirement.

Finally, establish what the numerator and denominator buy in deployment. The evaluator credits decoded mode capacity; that is useful traffic only under an adequate offered load. If excess capacity above demand is counted as delivered work, a high-mode optimum can be economically fictitious. Preserve the declared capacity result, and separately evaluate demand-limited goodput when relevant. If every protected user receives a fixed credited workload, maximizing efficiency reduces to minimizing energy for that workload.

The reported PA dominance is also dominance within the declared energy boundary: the [energy source explicitly excludes bus power and uses zero primary idle power](/home/sat/mcrl-v025-ladder-ws/src/mcrl/physics_v025/energy.py:87). It is not automatically whole-spacecraft energy dominance. Proposed OFF actions need special care: the dense endpoint counts baseband activity from scheduled transmissions, whereas scalar energy activates satellites using positive RF power. [Dense accounting](/home/sat/mcrl-v025-ladder-ws/src/mcrl/physics_v025/batch.py:491), [scalar accounting](/home/sat/mcrl-v025-ladder-ws/src/mcrl/physics_v025/energy.py:115). A new zero-power action must not profit from choosing whichever accounting path is cheaper.

Before acting on the number, require one inspectable record containing matched reference reproduction, raw pooled bits/joules, the service ledger, information restrictions, cap-stratum exposure, solver bounds and a frozen causal baseline’s held-out performance. Eight anchors can expose a defect; they cannot alone establish deployment prevalence or narrow uncertainty by counting repeated users, slots and boundaries as independent samples. Preserve negative outcomes and use the genuinely independent sampling unit for uncertainty. The owner has already rejected a one per cent result: judge the residual benefit after these controls against a prospectively stated practical threshold, rather than letting an encouraging oracle percentage choose the research direction.


FILE C1C2FIX-CODEX-2026-09-10.md 
 # C2 is **BOTH an encoder-adapter bug and a sufficiency gap**; an admissible one-scalar C1 repair exists, but it adds zero held-out predictive power.

**ADMISSIBILITY:** a feature is admissible only if it is computable from the pre-decision network state and the focal candidate action alone, without evaluating the candidate configuration's coupled power fixed point or its whole-network outcome, and without any label, oracle, or future information.

`DIAGNOSTIC_NOT_CLAIM`

No learner training or production run was performed. No threshold, sign, seed, horizon, price, service guard, acceptance rule, or `PILOT_PRIMITIVE_SOURCE_FALLBACK` was changed. The sealed clone and its virtual environment were not modified; its Python executable was used read-only with `PYTHONPATH=src` and `nice -n 15`.

## A. The C2 survival defect

### Finding

`state.py` does not compute survival, and the serializer is not overwriting or defaulting the flag. `extract_source_rows` appends `float(forecast.valid)` and `float(forecast.survival)` verbatim (`src/mcrl/stagec_v025/state.py:426-433`). The defect is upstream in the legacy pilot's primitive encoder adapter:

- `_build_anchor_rows_primitive` defines survival as `future_metric["legal"] and identity is not None`, then writes that same value to both `valid` and `survival` (`scripts/run_v025_pilot_c3.py:416-430`). `_primitive_metrics` defines `legal` only from future visibility, D2 eligibility, and cell reachability (`scripts/run_v025_pilot_c3.py:323-352`). It never asks whether the coupled evaluator serves the user.
- The exact projection uses a different predicate. `_batched_stage2_forecasts` evaluates the whole configuration, forms `changed = {u: candidate_mapping[u] != future_transition[u]}`, and requires **every changed user** to be in `profile.score.served_phy` (`.scratch/multi-catfish-v025-physics-successor/probe/run_v025_matrix_probe.py:1612-1638`).
- The non-fallback source builder would pass those exact `row.valid` and `row.survives` values into `ForecastObservation` correctly (`scripts/run_v025_pilot_c3.py:665-672`). Therefore this is specifically an adapter-path semantic misencoding, not a mutation in `state.py`.

For the real witness, both anchors have the same physical step-0 tape, current base mapping, incumbent mapping, and focal candidate `(user=6, action=(65028,44))`. Nevertheless, the future carrier transition makes the target predicate range over 93 changed users at each offset for anchor 0, but only the focal user for anchor 1. Thus the target uses the same predicate on different `changed` inputs across anchors, while the primitive encoder uses an entirely different predicate.

| Anchor | Stored Q2 `survival[1:3]` | Exact projection `survives[1:3]` | Exact C2 |
|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| 0, `nearest-eligible` | `(1,1,1)` | `(0,0,0)` | `60102789335062397912351470235270 / 11110569965387252983357173269901` = `5.4095145003632172...` |
| 1, `stay-if-possible` | `(1,1,1)` | `(1,1,1)` | `2149528301326970807719944431070731 / 111105699653872529833571732699010` = `19.346696956352321...` |

The absorbing rule is applied to the target but not to the encoded row. `c2_persistence_forecast` maintains `alive`; a first invalid/non-surviving projection makes every later offset a loss (`src/mcrl/physics_v025/targets.py:289-310`). Q2 stores three independent supplied booleans and has no cumulative `alive` transform. In this witness the distinction begins at offset 1, so anchor 0 incurs all three losses.

Classification: **BOTH**.

1. **ENCODER_BUG:** the primitive adapter labels geometric legality as both validity and coupled survival, although Q2 advertises distinct `valid` and `survival` fields. This is the immediate reason the stored row misreports all three flags.
2. **SUFFICIENCY_GAP:** the exact target flag is genuinely unavailable under the governing admissibility rule. It depends on a future carrier transition and a candidate whole-configuration coupled served set. The two witness inputs are identical at decision time, so no admissible deterministic feature can distinguish their target values. This also exposes a target-context defect: unrelated changed users can decide a focal action's “survival.”

Across the two cheap anchors, 822 of 1,784 valid nonreference rows have at least one stored-vs-exact **survival** mismatch (792/892 at anchor 0 and 30/892 at anchor 1), totalling 2,406 offset flags. Additionally, 200 rows have validity mismatches (600 offset flags). Counting a row affected by either boolean gives 1,022/1,784 rows. These anchors contain the previously established 62 exact C2 collision groups.

### Minimal real-tape reproduction

```text
nice -n 15 env PYTHONPATH=src \
  /home/sat/mcrl-leo-handover/.venv/bin/python \
  scripts/diagnose_v025_c1c2_sufficiency.py --survival-repro
```

The focused command completes in about 31 seconds and asserts byte-identical Q1/Q2, identical current base mappings, stored survival `[(true,true,true),(true,true,true)]`, and exact survival `[(false,false,false),(true,true,true)]`. It prints the exact fractions above and changed-user counts `[(93,93,93),(1,1,1)]`.

## B. Smallest admissible feature sets

Feature choice was declared before measurement. No second variant was tried.

| Route | Proposed feature | Exact definition and production supplier | Inputs | Admissibility, individually | Witnesses broken; encoded values |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| C1 | `predecision_network_max_rf_over_cap` | `p_max(base) / BEAM_RF_CAP_W`, where `p_max(base)` is `EvaluatedProfile.required_power_w_max`. `_base_configuration(tape, step_index, carrier)` supplies the committed configuration; `StepEvaluator.evaluate(base)` supplies the already-present network maximum (`.scratch/.../run_v025_matrix_probe.py:312-328,380-404`; `scripts/run_v025_pilot_c3.py:550-569`). | Decision-time tape, committed/base association, current setting; constant cap `1.65 W`. The focal candidate is not evaluated. | **Admissible.** It summarizes the current coupled state only. A controller may read/cache the already-committed power state before considering candidates. It contains no candidate outcome, label, oracle, or future value. | Breaks constructed C1 only: A `0.8/1.65 = 16/33 = 0.484848...`; B `1.5/1.65 = 10/11 = 0.909090...`. The repaired Q1 rows differ in their 17th scalar. It does not break the real C2 pair. |
| C2 | **No feature exists under the constraint** | Any deterministic function of decision-time state plus `(user 6,(65028,44))` receives identical inputs in both anchors. | Identical current tape/mapping/incumbent and candidate action. | Adding carrier identity, the future transition's changed-user set, or `_batched_stage2_forecasts(...).survives` is **inadmissible**: the first is source-policy context rather than network state, and the latter two use future/candidate coupled information. | Breaks neither; the admissible set remains empty. The tempting exact flag would give `(0,0,0)` vs `(1,1,1)`, but it is explicitly rejected as non-deployable. |

Cardinality one is minimal for C1 because zero added features preserves the published collision. C2 has no admissible separating set because the admissible inputs themselves are equal.

## C. Held-out predictive power

The rerun used `scripts/diagnose_v025_c1c2_sufficiency.py --anchors 30`, seed `20260909`, and the same physical-step split: train `1,2,4,6,7,8,9`; validation `5`; test `0,3`. It retained 26,345 exact-relabelled nonreference rows; the test set has 5,280 rows. Each deployable row used standardized inverse-distance k-NN with `k` selected from `{1,3,7,15,31}` on validation; `k=31` was selected throughout.

| Route | Feature row | Held-out EV | RMSE | Change in EV |
|---|---|---:|---:|---:|
| C1 | current Q1 only | `-0.3355476799` | `3.4683937281` | — |
| C1 | Q1 + one admissible feature | `-0.3355476799` | `3.4683937281` | `0.0000000000` |
| C2 | current Q2 only | `0.2106516781` | `3.3856461561` | — |
| C2 | admissible repair (empty; Q2 unchanged) | `0.2106516781` | `3.3856461561` | `0.0000000000` |
| C1 | `LEAKY_REFERENCE_NOT_DEPLOYABLE` | `1.0000000000` | `2.6554e-13` | not comparable |
| C2 | `LEAKY_REFERENCE_NOT_DEPLOYABLE` | `1.0000000000` | `1.0200e-14` | not comparable |

The admissible C1 repair buys **no measured predictive power**. Its value is exactly `1.0` at all 30 corpus anchors because every current base configuration reaches the RF cap, so standardization removes it. This negative result is not evidence that the feature is wrongly chosen for identifiability; it shows that breaking one contract witness does not supply variation useful on this corpus. C2 is unchanged because no admissible separator exists.

The leaky rows are only algebra checks. C1 OLS receives candidate/default whole-network bits, energy, and Phi; C2 OLS receives per-offset candidate/default outcome terms plus expanded absorbing state. Their `EV=1` merely recovers the target definitions and says nothing about deployable prediction.

## D. Consequences

### Expected pooled-energy-efficiency effect (under 200 words)

C1 cannot presently be expected to raise pooled energy efficiency. Its Q-only held-out EV is worse than the mean predictor, and the smallest admissible collision-breaking feature is constant on this sample and changes nothing. The exact C1 label is structurally sensitive to coupled whole-network effects that the action-local row still does not represent. A training run would not repair missing information and is not authorized by this diagnostic.

C2 also cannot presently be expected to raise pooled energy efficiency. Although Q2's held-out EV is positive (`0.2107`), its stored survival semantics are wrong on 822/1,784 two-anchor rows, and the exact target varies with future carrier-transition context absent from admissible deployment inputs. Correcting offline flags by copying coupled forecast outcomes would be leakage, not a deployable encoder repair. Before any efficacy claim, the focal-survival target must be made a function of admissible decision-time information (or the per-user C2 head design reconsidered), then evaluated on a fresh untouched split. No pooled-EE conclusion follows here.

### Minimum pipeline change and sealed-lineage stop (under 200 words)

For C1 only, `src/mcrl/stagec_v025/state.py` would need a v2 Q1 `FeatureSpec`, an `ActionEvaluation` field, and extraction of the 17th scalar. `scripts/run_v025_pilot_c3.py` would populate it from the cached decision-time `base_profile.required_power_w_max / BEAM_RF_CAP_W` in both source-builder paths. `tests/stagec_v025/test_stagec_pipeline.py` and relevant state/shard tests would need the new ordered width/schema assertions; synthetic rows already derive width from `Q1_FEATURES` but fixtures must be checked. `scripts/diagnose_v025_c1c2_sufficiency.py` contains the diagnostic-only measurement implemented here.

This changes `Q1_SCHEMA_SHA256`. Existing sealed JSONL shard headers, merged manifests, trained checkpoints, and frozen artifact digests would no longer match and must **not** be edited in place; adoption requires a newly versioned, newly generated artifact/manifest/checkpoint lineage. C2 additionally requires a target-contract decision, not an encoder-width change. Per the stop rule, no production schema, sealed artifact, frozen manifest, or training pipeline was modified.


